### Responsibility Matrix by Service Model

The following table summarizes the general responsibility split across various cloud service models:

| Layer / Control Area | IaaS (EC2) | PaaS (RDS/ECS) | FaaS (Lambda) | SaaS |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| **Physical facilities** | AWS | AWS | AWS | AWS |
| **Hardware / core network** | AWS | AWS | AWS | AWS |
| **Hypervisor** | AWS | AWS | AWS | AWS |
| **OS patching / hardening** | Customer | Mostly AWS | AWS | AWS |
| **Runtime / middleware** | Customer | Service-specific | AWS | AWS |
| **Application code** | Customer | Customer | Customer | Provider-managed *(Config matters)* |
| **Identity and access (IAM)** | Customer | Customer | Customer | Customer |
| **Data classification & access control** | Customer | Customer | Customer | Customer |
| **Encryption choices & settings** | Customer | Service-specific | Customer | Service-specific |
| **Network exposure** | Customer | Service-specific | Service-specific | Tenant Config |
| **Logging / monitoring configuration** | Customer | Customer / Service-specific | Customer | Customer / Service-specific |

---

### AWS Service Examples Breakdown

- **EC2:** The customer patches and hardens the operating system, configures Security Groups, manages IAM roles, and secures applications.
- **RDS:** AWS manages the underlying infrastructure and database engine maintenance. Customers manage database access, encryption choices, parameter settings, and network exposure.
- **Lambda:** AWS manages the infrastructure and execution environment; customers secure function code, permissions, secrets, and logging.
- **S3:** AWS secures the underlying infrastructure; customers configure bucket policies, access control lists (ACLs), public-access settings, encryption, and data lifecycle controls.

