## Task 3 - Real-World Example: Capital One - Cloud Misuse Pattern

### Incident Summary
A major breach occurred after an attacker accessed data stored in cloud storage by abusing a web-facing misconfiguration to steal temporary credentials.

### Root Cause
The attacker exploited a Server-Side Request Forgery (**SSRF**) vulnerability in a publicly reachable component (a misconfigured Web Application Firewall/app layer) to force the application to make requests to the EC2 Instance Metadata Service (IMDS).

The metadata endpoint returned temporary IAM role credentials. The attacker used these credentials via AWS APIs to access sensitive data stored in S3 buckets that the role was overly permitted to reach.

> ⚠️ **Takeaway:** This demonstrates a shared-responsibility failure: AWS operated the cloud infrastructure correctly, but customer-side application vulnerabilities and excessive IAM permissions created a path from an internet-facing app to credential theft and data exfiltration.

